00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.700
but addressing customers that
may be in particular regions.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.680
You can also do things,
like have functions

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.340
with the same name in different
regions in the same project.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.970
MYLES BORINS: So we
mentioned the new runtimes

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:19.710
for App Engine.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:22.240
But there is also some
runtime announcements for us

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.740
to make for Cloud Functions.

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.530
First of all, I know I'm
really excited about Node 8

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:28.600
for Cloud Functions.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:31.300
So you can now, as of
today, write Cloud Functions

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.620
using Node 8.11, which brings
in support for async await.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.420
For myself, that
was a major change

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.100
to the way in which
I wrote JavaScript,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:41.019
thought about asynchronicity.

00:05:41.019 --> 00:05:43.060
It also has a new function
signature, which we'll

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:44.380
be showing you in a minute.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.650
If you want to deploy, you
use the runtime Node.js.8.

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:49.400
Or if you're doing
it through the UI,

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.250
it's available through
the dropdown menu.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:55.120
So if you're writing
functions today with Node 6,

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.110
you may recognize this.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:58.960
This is what you would have for
a background function, where

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.630
the top one is a function
wrote with callback.

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:03.400
And the second one
is a function that's

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.830
able to return a promise.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:08.500
The API has changed a
little bit for Node 8,

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:12.130
where you could see we now
have a data and a context,

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:13.430
as opposed to an event.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:15.430
Previously, you would
have to dig into the event

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:16.870
to get all this information.

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:18.610
This is just available for you.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:19.810
It's a lot faster.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:22.750
You can provide async
functions as the function

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:24.550
itself, which allows
you to then use

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.410
the await keyword
to await the results

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:28.450
of asynchronous operations.

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:30.700
So as we can see, in
this async function

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.814
that we have here at the
top for hello_pubsub,

00:06:32.814 --> 00:06:34.730
we can do a whole bunch
of asynchronous stuff.

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:37.021
And if we had multiple of
these asynchronous operations

00:06:37.021 --> 00:06:39.070
in a row, we would
actually be able to do

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:41.590
those in a linear
pattern, which is,

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:43.600
for myself, a much
better mental model.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:45.400
Below, we can see the
function signature

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:48.910
for HTTP functions,
which remains unchanged.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:50.470
The main difference
being, again, you

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:52.960
can provide async functions
and use the await keyword

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.750
within it.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:58.140
So Python 3.7 is really
exciting as a new runtime.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.630
You can write your Cloud
Functions using Python.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:03.480
You deploy using
runtime Python 3.7.

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:08.190
Here's an example of HTTP
in a Pub/Sub function.

00:07:08.190 --> 00:07:10.770
You get a data and a context
for a Pub/Sub function

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:13.310
and a request for hello_http.

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:16.650
This is based on Flask.

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:18.431
A lot of people
in here use Flask?

00:07:18.431 --> 00:07:18.930
Cool.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:24.520
Yeah, really nice minimal
idiomatic Python way of writing

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:25.740
HTTP servers.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.100
You can do GET, PUT,
POST, DELETE, OPTIONS.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:31.580
The requests themselves are
based on Flask requests.

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:33.480
So if you know the
API signature of that,

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.430
you already know how
to get up and running.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.620
And if you don't,
the docs are there.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:39.540
And the responses just
need to be compatible

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:41.051
with Flask make response.

00:07:41.051 --> 00:07:43.050
And you'll be able to
start getting functions up

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:45.850
and running with Python today.

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:50.020
Python background
functions-- so as with Node,

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:53.320
you get the data, which,
in Python, is a dict.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.990
And then you get a context,
which is Google Cloud Functions

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:57.490
context.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:59.490
And that context is a
whole bunch of information

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:01.360
that you can dig in.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:04.180
To signal that the function
is successful in Python,

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.310
all you need to do is
return from your function.

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:09.250
And if there's any problems,
you raise an exception.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:10.810
And Stackdriver
error handling will

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.250
get automatically notified.

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:14.805
And you'll be able
to do whatever

00:08:14.805 --> 00:08:16.180
you want to set
up in the process

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.020
to handle error recovery.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:22.090
So there are common features
between both of these.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:23.980
You have logs.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:26.324
So if you log out using the
standard ways of logging

00:08:26.324 --> 00:08:27.740
in both libraries,
those are going

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.350
to be sucked up by Stackdriver
and ready for you to dig in.

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:33.893
If you've ever used the UI
for deploying functions,

00:08:33.893 --> 00:08:36.059
if you've done it with Node,
where you can just kind

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:38.490
of click immediately
after running a test

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:40.140
and see the logs to
see if it worked,

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:43.380
is the exact same way you
can do it with Python.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.480
Uncaught exceptions,
as I mentioned before,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.880
are automatically handed
off to Stackdriver Logging

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.100
and Stackdriver Error Reporting.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:53.467
And similarly to
Node, Python also

00:08:53.467 --> 00:08:55.050
will automatically
do the installation

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:57.060
of all of your dependencies.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:08:59.040
For Node, you have
a package.json.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.410
For Python, you have
a requirements.txt.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:04.380
So just list your requirements
in the requirements.txt.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.780
And we are going to install
all your tendencies for you

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:09.310
in the cloud.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:11.380
The context object that
I mentioned before, this

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:12.700
is how it breaks down.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:15.790
You have the event ID, the
time stamp, the event type,

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.600
and the resource.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:20.140
These will be only for
background functions.

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:23.020
You don't get a context
object with HTTP functions.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.330
For those, you get a request
and a response in Node.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:27.925
And you get the
request for Python.

00:09:27.925 --> 00:09:29.800
But these are the options
that you would have

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:31.060
from a background function.

00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:33.600
And you can use this in
your code to determine,

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.530
was it Pub/Sub
that triggered me,

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:38.062
or am I being triggered
by another event?

00:09:38.062 --> 00:09:39.520
But everything is
in there that you

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:41.650
need to understand the
context of the object

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:43.870
and where it's running.

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:46.360
This is an example of
what that may look like.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:49.525
This would be for a publish
event on a Pub/Sub queue.

00:09:49.525 --> 00:09:52.150
And you can get all the way down
to even the version of Pub/Sub

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.550
if you're breaking down
that string in the type.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:56.080
So a lot of
information and context

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:58.830
that you can get from this.

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:02.070
For those of you who are
using Firebase functions,

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:05.310
Cloud Functions for
Firebase is now also in GA.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:07.020
And it has full
support for Node 8,

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.150
so you can write
your ECMAScript 2017.

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.740
You can use async await.

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:14.400
The language features that are
supported by GCF also supported

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.350
by Firebase functions.

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:18.360
There's new runtime
configuration options

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:21.140
that allow you to control your
region and your memory and time

00:10:21.140 --> 00:10:21.780
out.

00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:25.380
These are really great granular
controls and a great thing

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:28.539
to dig into for the productivity
of your applications.

00:10:28.539 --> 00:10:31.080
One of the really cool things
that's coming with this launch,

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.390
too, is that Firebase
events for analytics

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:36.637
files store realtime, DB,
and authentication are now

00:10:36.637 --> 00:10:38.220
available directly
in Cloud Functions.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.320
So you can use your
Firebase events

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:43.604
to trigger your Cloud Functions.

00:10:43.604 --> 00:10:46.020
Both of these different products
have different great ways

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:46.920
of using them.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.390
And now, you can,
depending on what

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:50.010
you're doing with your
stack, make decisions

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:51.635
based on what works
best for your team.

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:57.096
JASON POLITES: Thanks.

00:10:57.096 --> 00:10:58.970
One of the things that
we talked about before

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:03.140
in the new runtimes
for App Engine, this

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:04.520
applies to that as well.

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:08.570
We're rebasing the underlying
operating system on Ubuntu.

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:11.150
And one of the main
reasons for doing this

00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:16.150
is our ability to provide
system libraries and native

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:17.420
binaries in the image.

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:19.610
In Cloud Functions,
historically, we've

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:22.800
really only just allowed
one, and that's ImageMagick.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:24.650
With this switch
to Ubuntu, we're

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:26.154
really broadening the scope.

00:11:26.154 --> 00:11:27.320
I've listed a couple there--

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:28.580
ImageMagick, FF MPEG.

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:29.955
This is something
a lot of people

00:11:29.955 --> 00:11:31.700
have asked for video processing.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:33.200
And we've also made
sure that we've

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:35.300
bundled in all the
system libraries required

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:36.980
to run Headless Chrome.

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:40.100
So you can take
screenshots of a web page

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:41.990
from within a Cloud Function.

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:44.420
And if you want
the big list, here

00:11:44.420 --> 00:11:46.730
is the list as of
a few weeks ago.

00:11:46.730 --> 00:11:49.850
It may have been actually
added to since then.

00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:52.700
I won't spend too
much time on that.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:54.950
Another feature that a lot
of customers have asked for

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:57.440
is environment variables.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:00.230
We're announcing
today that you can now

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:04.555
specify key value pairs that
are bound to a single function.

00:12:04.555 --> 00:12:06.430
But they don't exist
inside your source code.

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:09.650
You set them at deploy
time, just any arbitrary

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:11.400
number of key value pairs.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:13.700
And then those will be
injected as literal environment

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:16.140
variables at execution time.

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:18.410
So at deploy time,
we'll save those.

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:20.240
And then at
execution time, we'll

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:22.837
inject those into
the environment.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:29.970
This is available in the
web UI, the cloud console.

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:31.930
You can just set them in there.

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:34.200
It's also available
in the API and the CLI

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.630
you saw just before.

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:39.050
OK, Myles, demo.

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:39.800
MYLES BORINS: Yes.

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:43.370
So all the things we just
talked about, I decided, hey,

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:46.860
let's make something silly that
uses all of them all at once,

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:48.770
so I can show you
some things on stage.

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:51.470
I've got a demo
here on my machine

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:54.500
that I'm going to show you.

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:55.880
We got here the Math Bot.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:57.890
It's really friendly,
nice person.

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:00.260
I'm going to start a new
conversation with the Math Bot.

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:06.050
And I'll just be, like, hey,
Math Bot, what's 1 plus 1?

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:07.460
And Math Both needs to think.

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:08.270
It's been sleeping.

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:10.814
So if you're not familiar
with scale to zero,

00:13:10.814 --> 00:13:13.230
when you're not using your
functions, they're not running.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:14.547
So you're not paying for it.

00:13:14.547 --> 00:13:16.880
It also means that you have
what's known as a cold boot.

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:18.434
The first time that
you do something,

00:13:18.434 --> 00:13:19.850
it's going to take
a second for it

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:21.860
to spin up, and figure
everything out, and get

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:24.490
running.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:27.430
Hey, Math Bot.

00:13:27.430 --> 00:13:29.710
Maybe I shouldn't have
picked something so hard.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:30.610
Oh, I know why.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:33.640
Because I didn't add Math Bot.

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:35.029
Didn't know I was talking to it.

00:13:35.029 --> 00:13:36.820
And this is actually
a really great feature

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:37.630
for Hangouts Chat.

00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:39.010
So if you're making
bots, they're

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:41.530
not just going to be responding
to anything that you're

00:13:41.530 --> 00:13:42.640
saying inside of the chat.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:45.980
They're only going to actually
respond when you talk to them.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:47.849
We've got tutorials
available online.

00:13:47.849 --> 00:13:50.140
So you can follow and get
your own Hangouts bots going.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:52.520
So we could see Math Bot got
back to me pretty quickly.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:53.890
I could just be like, what's pi?

00:13:56.207 --> 00:13:57.790
It's not going to
say anything smarmy.

00:13:57.790 --> 00:13:59.800
It's going to be
pretty quick on that.

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:01.134
And while I'm waiting for that--

00:14:01.134 --> 00:14:03.008
JASON POLITES: It's not
going to say anything

00:14:03.008 --> 00:14:04.009
if you don't address it.

00:14:04.009 --> 00:14:04.966
MYLES BORINS: Oh, yeah.

00:14:04.966 --> 00:14:05.762
I keep doing that.

00:14:05.762 --> 00:14:06.470
So you know what?

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:08.303
I'm just going to go
to Math Bot right here.

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:12.810
I'll be like, what's pi?

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:18.050
And it'll say, hopefully, 3.14.

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:19.800
I can be like, what's
the meaning of life?

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:26.664
And, of course, it's
going to come back--

00:14:26.664 --> 00:14:29.280
JASON POLITES: It's
navigating the Wi-Fi.

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:33.400
MYLES BORINS: It's getting
really esoteric with me.

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:36.190
So what we've got going on
here are two Cloud Functions.

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:38.530
We've got a Node gs Cloud
Function and a Python Cloud

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:40.340
Function doing two
separate things.

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:42.200
So I have my own expertise.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:43.880
And one of them is
not machine learning.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:45.070
I know Node really well.

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:47.710
So when I want it to start
instrumenting with the Hangouts

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:51.590
Chat API and I need it to do
things I haven't done before,

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:53.530
it was way more idiomatic
and straightforward

00:14:53.530 --> 00:14:55.240
for me to get started with Node.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:58.300
So I used Node to create
a bridge between Hangouts

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:00.130
Chat and Cloud Functions.

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:02.390
But I don't know anything
about writing chat bots.

00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:03.580
But I was Googling
around and found

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:05.038
this thing called
ChatterBot, which

00:15:05.038 --> 00:15:06.850
is available on PIP,
which had a demo that

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:08.240
worked right out of the box.

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:10.570
So I was able to get a chat
bot spun up with Python.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:12.069
So if you take a
look here, we could

00:15:12.069 --> 00:15:16.150
see here's the Hangouts bot.

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:18.730
Here's the source code for
the node side of things.

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:20.200
And we're using Node Fetch.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:21.340
I'm a big fan of fetch.

00:15:21.340 --> 00:15:23.710
It fetches an API for
making network requests

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:25.240
that returns promise.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.940
We've got this function,
which is an async function

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:31.450
to ask the bot, which makes
the body, sets up the headers.

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:33.850
It does a tri-catch,
where it goes and waits

00:15:33.850 --> 00:15:36.430
for the response
from the bot URL.

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:39.190
This is, as we can see up
here, an environment variable

00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:41.464
that we've injected
inside of the function.

00:15:41.464 --> 00:15:42.880
And we wait for
the response text,

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:45.160
which is resolving
the result of that.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.604
And the rest of
this is the function

00:15:47.604 --> 00:15:49.270
that we're handing
out to Hangouts Chat.

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:53.410
It's just saying, hey, grab
the message and ask the bot.

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:55.270
And then asking the bot
is heading over here

00:15:55.270 --> 00:15:57.850
to the Python code,
which we can see,

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:00.460
which is about 41 lines of code.

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.820
And it's mostly just
instantiation code,

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:04.900
where I've set up
two logic adapters.

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:06.400
One of them is
custom, so I can make

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.410
that joke about the meaning
of life that failed.

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:11.020
And then the other one is
the mathematics evaluation

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:12.160
in there.

00:16:12.160 --> 00:16:14.200
And it was pretty
straightforward to tie the two

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:14.740
together.

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:18.790
If we take a look here back
in the code and we click Edit,

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:23.170
we can quickly take a look
at the environment variables

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:24.640
that we've got down here.

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:26.920
And we can see that I've
got the bot URL right here.

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:28.540
And that's just the URL
of the other function

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:29.620
that I'm going to call.

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:31.150
So I didn't need to
embed that right in.

00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:32.900
If I change the
background function there,

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:35.502
I can quickly get
that going later.

00:16:35.502 --> 00:16:37.210
But what we've seen
here is a combination

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:39.130
of using Node for
what Node's good for,

00:16:39.130 --> 00:16:41.204
using Python for
what's Python good for,

00:16:41.204 --> 00:16:43.120
and using environment
variables to make things

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:44.680
a little bit more flexible.

00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:46.880
And then all of this
plugs into Hangouts Chat,

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:48.970
so that I don't
need to calculate

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:51.760
how many grams of coffee to
use when I'm doing my 1:13

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:53.080
ratio of pourover.

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:56.492
I can just ask Math Bot, and
it can get snarky with me.

00:16:56.492 --> 00:16:57.950
But so that's the
end of this demo.

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:00.770
We can head back to
the slide deck now.

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:03.410
And I think it's
back to you, Jason.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:05.346
JASON POLITES: I think so.

00:17:05.346 --> 00:17:05.846
Thanks.

00:17:11.849 --> 00:17:14.400
So we'll just keep on
rolling with the new things

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:15.869
that we're adding.

00:17:15.869 --> 00:17:19.159
For those people out there
who have used Cloud SQL,

00:17:19.159 --> 00:17:20.700
you may have bumped
into this problem

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:22.410
that we had with
Cloud Functions,

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:25.560
where it was pretty difficult
to talk to Cloud SQL

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:29.640
without doing a U-turn out
to the internet and back.

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:31.110
So we fixed that.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:34.680
You now have a direct
connection to Cloud SQL.

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:37.500
You just use this socket
path that you can see there--

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:41.610
slash cloud SQL. /foo is just
the name of my database there.

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:44.820
This would just be
in your Node code.

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:46.490
In this case, it's JavaScript.

00:17:46.490 --> 00:17:50.280
There's a little thing there
you may have picked up on.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:53.490
This is creating a
connection pool of size 1.

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:55.680
Why do I need a
pool if it's only 1?

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:59.220
And that's really a
side effect of the fact

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:00.800
that it's a serverless
environment.

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:05.790
It's going to scale up and down
from 0 and up to some number

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:07.020
and then back to 0.

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:09.480
And so if you're
scaling up rapidly--

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:14.460
and I'll talk a little bit
more about this in a minute--

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:15.960
you might overwhelm
your database.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:18.540
If each function is creating
10 database connections,

00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:21.390
and we do the magic of
scaling it up for you,

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:24.420
and suddenly, you [INAUDIBLE]
your own database.

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:26.499
So we recommend a
connection size of 1.

00:18:26.499 --> 00:18:28.290
But we recommend you
use a pool, because it

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:30.950
has some nice features,
like auto re-establishing

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:33.970
your connection if it fails.

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:35.910
So this was the previous world.

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:40.200
We would ask customers to do
this horrendous U-turn out

00:18:40.200 --> 00:18:42.840
to the internet and use
SSL to secure it and so on.

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:44.070
Well, no more of that.

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:45.900
Now, you can just
go straight across.

00:18:45.900 --> 00:18:48.030
And that's a secure
connection managed by us.

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:54.175
As I was just talking before,
scaling controls is a new thing

00:18:54.175 --> 00:18:55.530
that we're adding.

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:57.660
This is exactly the
scenario I just described.

00:18:57.660 --> 00:19:00.390
You have some nice
evenly-shaped traffic,

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:03.730
notwithstanding the fact
that my diagram is not even.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:06.090
Traffic's coming in, the
database is happy, everyone's

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:06.840
happy.

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:08.460
You get some more traffic.

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:11.170
And we do a great job
of scaling up for you.

00:19:11.170 --> 00:19:13.920
But then, of course, your
database is overloaded.

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:15.880
And you can't deal
with anymore traffic.

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:17.920
So what you do in
this situation?

00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:19.780
Well, we're introducing
scaling controls.

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:23.730
So you can actually limit
the number of instances

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:26.271
that your function
is going to use.

00:19:26.271 --> 00:19:27.520
Why would you want to do that?

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:29.186
Why would you want
to limit the traffic?

00:19:29.186 --> 00:19:32.920
Well, the simple
canonical example is,

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:35.850
let's say you have one
database and two functions.

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:38.639
And one function is your really
important production function

00:19:38.639 --> 00:19:39.930
that you don't want to go down.

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:42.622
And maybe it's what your
mobile clients are calling.

00:19:42.622 --> 00:19:43.830
And that's the important one.

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:46.770
And then maybe you have some
other background function

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:49.350
or some other analytics
or reporting thing

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:52.200
that it doesn't really
matter if it doesn't succeed.

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:55.530
And maybe it has a bug,
and it gets into a loop.

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:58.260
And it causes us to
scale up many instances

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:00.947
of this non-important function.

00:20:00.947 --> 00:20:02.530
And it causes the
database to go down,

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:05.470
which causes your really
important function to cease

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:05.970
operating.

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:08.250
So in this case, you
would put scaling controls

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:09.990
over that less
important function

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:11.670
to preserve the
database to make sure

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:16.696
that the traffic is prioritized
for the more important one.

00:20:16.696 --> 00:20:19.479
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:19.479 --> 00:20:20.770
JASON POLITES: I'm sorry, what?

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:22.210
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:23.040
JASON POLITES: Yes.

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.720
Or you could use API throttling.

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:27.510
The comment was, or if there's
a throttling limit in the API,

00:20:27.510 --> 00:20:30.830
yes, that would
also be appropriate.

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:36.010
The limits are per function,
default limit of up to 1,000.

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:42.050
And then you can change
it on per function basis.

00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:44.390
So this is what it looks like
with the scaling control,

00:20:44.390 --> 00:20:46.430
same sort of situation.

00:20:46.430 --> 00:20:50.910
Regular traffic coming
in, but now, new traffic.

00:20:50.910 --> 00:20:53.405
The gray vertical line is
representing the boundary

00:20:53.405 --> 00:20:55.280
of your scaling limit
that you've said, well,

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:57.020
I don't want to scale
beyond this limit.

00:20:57.020 --> 00:21:01.730
So those boxes with the dotted
line will not be created

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:02.980
and your database stays happy.

00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:10.360
MYLES BORINS: For myself,
I've wanted scheduling

00:21:10.360 --> 00:21:11.810
for a really long time.

00:21:11.810 --> 00:21:13.090
I don't know about all of you.

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:17.980
But we're really excited to talk
today about Cloud Scheduler.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:21.520
Cloud Scheduler allows you
to schedule HTTP or Pub/Sub

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:27.070
tasks at variables starting
down to 1 minute intervals.

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:31.720
It can invoke Cloud Functions
over HTTPS or over Pub/Sub.

00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:35.440
And it can also invoke App
Engine on a relative URL.

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:37.960
So this means that we can start
setting up timers for events

00:21:37.960 --> 00:21:42.280
that we want to happen,
like setting up chron jobs.

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:46.720
If you have tasks that need to
happen at a certain interval,

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:48.520
this is the way in
which you can set it up.

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:50.186
First, we're going
to take a look at how

00:21:50.186 --> 00:21:53.360
that looks for Cloud Functions.

00:21:53.360 --> 00:21:55.860
You create a Scheduler job.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:58.410
You name that job.

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:01.260
You have a message body, which
is what the body of the message

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:04.470
will be when it's triggered,
and you schedule it.

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:09.060
You can do that using just
an English-like grammar.

00:22:09.060 --> 00:22:10.620
So you say every one minute.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:12.390
You specify the
URL that it's going

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:14.220
to trigger and the
HTTP method that it's

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:15.450
going to use to trigger it.

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:18.720
So now, this endpoint is
going to get triggered

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:21.350
every single minute with post.

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:23.340
So if you have things
that you need to do,

00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:29.640
like checking an endpoint or
doing anything via batch job,

00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:31.410
you can schedule it this way.

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:33.452
For App Engine, it's a
little bit different.

00:22:33.452 --> 00:22:35.160
You'll notice the two
biggest differences

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:36.890
are the relative
URL in the service.

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:40.457
With App Engine, you can specify
the specific relative URL

00:22:40.457 --> 00:22:42.540
to the service that you're
going to be triggering.

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:44.970
And you can specify the service,
because with App Engine,

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:47.860
you can deploy multiple
services to the same project.

00:22:47.860 --> 00:22:50.370
So you don't necessarily want
to be triggering the default

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:51.570
service.

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:58.170
I believe that the schedule's
probably the only thing that's

00:22:58.170 --> 00:22:58.709
mandatory.

00:22:58.709 --> 00:23:00.750
But you can go through
the docs, and take a look,

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:04.260
and see exactly how you
got to spin this up.

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:05.230
But it's really great.

00:23:05.230 --> 00:23:06.720
It's intuitive.

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:08.860
And I really love
this capability,

00:23:08.860 --> 00:23:10.560
and I think it expands
lot of the things

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:15.010
that we can build with our
serverless offerings right now.

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:17.550
We're going to bring Dima
up on stage-- a quick round

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:18.862
of applause for Dima.

00:23:18.862 --> 00:23:20.630
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:23.970
And just don't worry
about the fact that it is

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:26.280
says product marketing on there.

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:27.592
Everyone at Google writes code.

00:23:27.592 --> 00:23:28.800
DMYTRO MELNYK: Thanks, Jason.

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:29.925
Thanks, Myles.

00:23:29.925 --> 00:23:32.067
I'll stick to this mic here.

00:23:32.067 --> 00:23:32.650
Hey, everyone.

00:23:32.650 --> 00:23:36.120
I'm Dima and I lead product
marketing for cloud functions.

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:37.800
And let me show
you a demo, which

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:39.510
I built using some
of those new features

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.390
that Jason and Myles
have just discussed.

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:43.770
Actually, I need some slides.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:49.570
Can we just please go back to
the slide deck for a moment?

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:51.960
Thank you.

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:54.590
So sometimes you
have to rely on--

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:58.200
thanks, Jason--
an external system

00:23:58.200 --> 00:24:01.620
as a part of your serverless
application architecture.

00:24:01.620 --> 00:24:03.840
I chose my SQL
database in this case.

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:07.240
But that could be any
third party API, really.

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:10.740
So imagine a scenario when
your database goes down.

00:24:10.740 --> 00:24:14.490
It could be due to an
outage or a serverless part

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:16.200
of your system scaling
out of proportion

00:24:16.200 --> 00:24:18.700
and overwhelming your database.

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:21.980
So you try to write something
into the database and it fails.

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:22.480
What's next?

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:31.250
You could actually
implement some retry logic.

00:24:31.250 --> 00:24:32.990
And you could retry
things immediately.

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:34.823
And this could help you
when you are dealing

00:24:34.823 --> 00:24:37.040
with a small database delay
or a small interruption

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:38.600
in connectivity.

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:44.360
But what if your database
is totally slammed

00:24:44.360 --> 00:24:46.460
and you have thousands
of messages that failed?

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:48.140
You might not want
to overwhelm it

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.930
with additional retries
every couple of seconds.

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:57.890
In that case, you can save your
fail messages to something,

00:24:57.890 --> 00:24:59.370
like Pub/Sub, for example.

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:00.900
But there is a
problem with Pub/Sub.

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:02.090
It's really fast.

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:03.710
And your retry
logic will actually

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:06.920
trigger right away,
possibly still too fast,

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:10.304
if the database is down for
an extended period of time.

00:25:10.304 --> 00:25:11.970
And obviously, if you
relied on Pub/Sub,

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:15.010
you actually would have
to re-queue those messages

00:25:15.010 --> 00:25:16.460
if they failed again.

00:25:16.460 --> 00:25:19.610
So when you deal with an
unknown amount of downtime,

00:25:19.610 --> 00:25:21.980
you might want to persist
those messages into something

00:25:21.980 --> 00:25:26.030
like Cloud storage, so that you
can replay them later, and take

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:28.130
your time processing them.

00:25:28.130 --> 00:25:30.680
So to trigger this
retry logic on schedule,

00:25:30.680 --> 00:25:33.129
you can actually stand up a VM.

00:25:33.129 --> 00:25:34.670
And you can create
a virtual machine,

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:38.690
and things like Linux crontab
have been around for, like,

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:40.070
50 years.

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:41.970
But we're doing serverless here.

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:44.700
And what if your current
server goes down?

00:25:44.700 --> 00:25:47.210
Do you have another
one watching?

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:49.640
This is where Cloud
Scheduler can really help.

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:51.626
So let's see it in action now.

00:25:51.626 --> 00:25:56.550
If we can please go
back to the demo slides.

00:25:56.550 --> 00:25:59.280
So what you're looking at
here is a front end part

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:00.210
of this demo.

00:26:00.210 --> 00:26:01.710
It's built on Firebase.

00:26:01.710 --> 00:26:03.390
And all it does,
it just visualizes

00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:05.340
messages moving between
different components

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:09.455
of the back end
architecture in real time.

00:26:09.455 --> 00:26:10.830
So what we're
going to the first,

00:26:10.830 --> 00:26:13.500
we will send some
load to the HTTP end

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:15.630
point, which is sitting
in front of the database.

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:19.115
And all it does, it writes some
messages to the MySQL database.

00:26:19.115 --> 00:26:20.490
And I just click
the task button,

00:26:20.490 --> 00:26:26.230
and it sends a few
requests to the database.

00:26:26.230 --> 00:26:28.630
It's a live demo, so there
is some latency involved.

00:26:28.630 --> 00:26:30.630
OK, there they are.

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:33.960
We see some blue and
green boxes appear here.

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:35.879
So the blue boxes,
they represent messages

00:26:35.879 --> 00:26:37.170
that were sent to the database.

00:26:37.170 --> 00:26:39.930
And the green ones are the
corresponding successful

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.730
writes.

00:26:41.730 --> 00:26:43.710
So when everything is
working as expected,

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:47.610
we would expect to see the equal
number of those boxes, which is

00:26:47.610 --> 00:26:50.130
exactly what we're seeing here.

00:26:50.130 --> 00:26:52.740
Now let me actually go
to my project settings

00:26:52.740 --> 00:26:55.660
and take the database down
to simulate an outage.

00:26:58.430 --> 00:27:01.690
I'm using Cloud SQL just for
the purpose of this demo.

00:27:01.690 --> 00:27:03.840
So what I'll do, I'll just
go ahead and restart it,

00:27:03.840 --> 00:27:06.470
and that usually
takes a few moments.

00:27:06.470 --> 00:27:08.100
And while the database
is restarting,

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:10.440
I'm going back to
the visualizer app

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:12.775
and sending a few more
requests to the database.

00:27:15.450 --> 00:27:20.230
So now, we see some of
the red boxes pop up here.

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:23.260
Those represent messages
that failed as expected,

00:27:23.260 --> 00:27:24.854
because the database is down.

00:27:24.854 --> 00:27:26.770
So those messages are
saved to the dead letter

00:27:26.770 --> 00:27:29.474
queue, which is built with
Pub/Sub and Cloud Storage.

00:27:29.474 --> 00:27:31.640
And I, of course, have
another Cloud Function there,

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:33.200
which I'm not showing
for simplicity.

00:27:33.200 --> 00:27:35.980
All it does, it just
saves the messages

00:27:35.980 --> 00:27:39.472
as they pop up on
the Pub/Sub topic.

00:27:39.472 --> 00:27:41.680
Now what we can do is we
can go ahead and take a look

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:43.450
at the dead letter
queue and also

00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:47.600
create a Cloud Scheduler job
to rerun those failed messages.

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:52.220
So this is my dead letter
queue cloud storage bucket.

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:55.220
And sometimes it takes a moment
for the UI to refresh here.

00:27:55.220 --> 00:27:57.839
So I'm just going to
click Refresh button.

00:27:57.839 --> 00:27:59.630
And we can see some of
our failed messages.

00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:02.360
They started to pop up here
with the appropriate time

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:04.430
stamps from a minute or so ago.

00:28:08.090 --> 00:28:10.060
After taking a look at
that, let's go ahead

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:12.910
and create a Cloud
Scheduler job.

00:28:12.910 --> 00:28:16.220
I actually pre-populated all
the values just to save time.

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:19.610
And as you can see, all you need
to do here, it's really simple.

00:28:19.610 --> 00:28:20.870
You just have to name it.

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:23.780
And I'm using Hello Next
18 here as the name.

00:28:23.780 --> 00:28:26.560
Set up frequency, such
as every Friday afternoon

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:29.440
or, as in our
case, every minute.

00:28:29.440 --> 00:28:31.000
And also select target.

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:33.650
In our case, the target is
the HTTP end point which

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:34.995
replace the failed messages.

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:40.100
And I'll also go ahead
and start a timer here

00:28:40.100 --> 00:28:42.110
to track approximately
how much time we

00:28:42.110 --> 00:28:44.904
have left until that Cloud
Scheduler job fires off.

00:28:44.904 --> 00:28:47.320
And as you can see here in the
console, it hasn't run yet.

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:51.120
It will very soon.

00:28:51.120 --> 00:28:54.530
I just want to reiterate
it was super-easy to create

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:56.246
a scheduled job with
Cloud Scheduler.

00:28:56.246 --> 00:28:58.370
So we actually accomplished
something very powerful

00:28:58.370 --> 00:28:59.670
here with just a few clicks.

00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:01.730
We now have this code that
will run every minute,

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:05.101
go to the dead letter queue, and
try to replay failed messages,

00:29:05.101 --> 00:29:05.600
if any.

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:11.610
OK, so I guess the Cloud
Scheduler job already

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:14.340
triggered.

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:17.110
From my initial test, I think it
happens on top of every minute.

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:20.380
And so that was an approximate
timer for dramatic effect.

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:24.990
But it already triggered and
actually successfully replayed

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:28.480
all of those failed messages.

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:30.750
And just to double check,
I'll go back to the bucket

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:31.800
and refresh it.

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:33.030
And it should be empty.

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:36.760
Yes, and it is
empty as expected.

00:29:36.760 --> 00:29:41.940
So just switching back
to the visualizer app.

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:45.150
As you could see, it
replayed successfully.

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:47.850
And in the real life
scenario, obviously,

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:50.360
if you're building
this for real,

00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:52.110
and you had a lot
of failed messages,

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:55.860
like, thousands of them, there
might be a situation that when

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:58.710
your dead letter queue holds
way more messages than you

00:29:58.710 --> 00:30:00.870
can actually replay
within the minute.

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:06.300
So that could be solved by
either increasing the time

00:30:06.300 --> 00:30:08.700
interval or introducing
another cloud storage

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:13.280
bucket to hold messages
that are being replayed.

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:15.330
And to recap, I just
talked about this idea

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:18.270
of how you can build a
reliable system using

00:30:18.270 --> 00:30:21.890
some of the primitives that we
have available to you on GCP

00:30:21.890 --> 00:30:23.130
today.

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:25.020
And this pattern can
be especially useful

00:30:25.020 --> 00:30:27.620
when you are relying
on external systems

00:30:27.620 --> 00:30:31.080
as a part of your
implementation.

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:33.066
Thank you and back
to Jason and Myles.

00:30:33.066 --> 00:30:35.496
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:38.234 --> 00:30:39.400
JASON POLITES: Thanks, Dima.

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:40.900
If we can go back
to slides, please.

00:30:40.900 --> 00:30:42.020
Oh, is this slides?

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:44.280
It looks the same.

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:47.515
OK, let's just keep rolling.

00:30:47.515 --> 00:30:48.730
More on Cloud Functions--

00:30:48.730 --> 00:30:52.120
Access and IAM.

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:58.360
We're also very excited
to announce VPC access.

00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:00.490
Again, if anyone's used
Cloud Functions before,

00:31:00.490 --> 00:31:04.150
you would have plausibly
bumped into this limitation.

00:31:04.150 --> 00:31:08.050
Consider you have some number
of virtual machines, GCE

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:10.960
instances in Cloud Platform.

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:14.709
Those instances, let's say,
are all on the same network.

00:31:14.709 --> 00:31:15.250
That's great.

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:16.930
They can talk to each other.

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:19.870
And then you have a Cloud
Function out here in the wild,

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:23.320
but it cannot talk to those
virtual machines for very

00:31:23.320 --> 00:31:27.450
irrational and complicated
network security reasons.

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:29.740
Good news is we fixed that bug.

00:31:29.740 --> 00:31:34.450
So you can now connect the
Cloud Function via a connection

00:31:34.450 --> 00:31:38.680
service to that
network A and, thereby,

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:42.670
grant access from the function
to any virtual machine

00:31:42.670 --> 00:31:43.420
on that network.

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:49.560
So you just simply add the
Cloud Function to the network,

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:52.860
and it allows you to
egress, to transmit bytes

00:31:52.860 --> 00:31:57.900
from the function to
the virtual machine.

00:31:57.900 --> 00:32:01.690
Very simple command line
at deploy time, connected

00:32:01.690 --> 00:32:04.540
VPC with the name of the
network, and you're done.

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:11.450
On the side of security
controls, something that we get

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:13.850
asked for a lot.

00:32:13.850 --> 00:32:16.580
When we started with Cloud
Functions, when we announced

00:32:16.580 --> 00:32:18.530
the beta last year,
I mentioned before

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:21.020
that one of the features
that we wanted to promote

00:32:21.020 --> 00:32:22.760
was this ability to
deploy a function

00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:25.610
and give it an HTTP URL
and curl it straight away.

00:32:25.610 --> 00:32:27.260
And it's very, very
good for simplicity

00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:29.270
and terrible for security.

00:32:29.270 --> 00:32:32.810
Because this HTTP
function is invokable,

00:32:32.810 --> 00:32:34.530
effectively, globally.

00:32:34.530 --> 00:32:38.150
So what we're introducing
now is IAM controls

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:41.400
on the data plane itself,
on the invoke of a function.

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:45.200
We have a new role called
Cloud Functions invoker,

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:47.480
and you can assign it
to a particular user.

00:32:47.480 --> 00:32:54.530
In the example above, I'm saying
Alice can invoke my function

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:56.360
code helloworld.

00:32:56.360 --> 00:32:59.670
And in the example below, that's
effectively making it public.

00:32:59.670 --> 00:33:02.630
We have a special string
in Cloud Platform called

00:33:02.630 --> 00:33:06.170
all users, which effectively
says that all users have invoke

00:33:06.170 --> 00:33:07.419
permission on my function.

00:33:07.419 --> 00:33:09.710
And this means you can now
deploy a Cloud Function that

00:33:09.710 --> 00:33:12.620
cannot be invoked by anyone
in the outside world.

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:15.560
And this is perfect for
function-to-function invocation

00:33:15.560 --> 00:33:16.190
use cases.

00:33:18.770 --> 00:33:20.180
It looks like this.

00:33:20.180 --> 00:33:22.970
Traffic from the outside world
comes into our serving stack.

00:33:22.970 --> 00:33:26.874
There's an IAM check, which
checks does this request have

00:33:26.874 --> 00:33:28.040
the appropriate credentials?

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:30.140
Are those credentials authentic?

00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:34.430
And if they are, the function
receives the request as normal.

00:33:34.430 --> 00:33:36.950
The same situation
applies if one function

00:33:36.950 --> 00:33:38.720
is calling another function.

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:42.770
Its identity will be verified
by this IAM check and its role

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:45.020
verified to make sure that
it has the appropriate role

00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:48.010
on that function.

00:33:48.010 --> 00:33:54.360
OK, that is the bulk of
the regular announcements.

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:57.240
For those who are
paying attention,

00:33:57.240 --> 00:33:59.520
the N is just
representing the number.

00:33:59.520 --> 00:34:01.260
It doesn't really have a number.

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:04.260
And that's because this
next bit is not ready yet.

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:05.550
This is a sneak peek.

00:34:05.550 --> 00:34:09.989
If anyone was in the
serverless spotlight earlier,

00:34:09.989 --> 00:34:12.810
you may have seen something
in relation to this.

00:34:12.810 --> 00:34:17.840
And those who weren't,
you get to see it now.

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:22.159
Coming very soon is
serverless containers.

00:34:22.159 --> 00:34:25.670
We talked a little bit before
about these next generation

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:26.810
runtimes in App Engine.

00:34:26.810 --> 00:34:29.389
We talked about
some commonalities

00:34:29.389 --> 00:34:31.400
in the infrastructure.

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:33.150
Underneath all of that--

00:34:33.150 --> 00:34:35.750
and this has been true
actually since day one

00:34:35.750 --> 00:34:37.400
of Cloud Functions--

00:34:37.400 --> 00:34:41.440
we actually except as an
input into the infrastructure

00:34:41.440 --> 00:34:42.942
a Docker image.

00:34:42.942 --> 00:34:44.400
And so what we're
going to be doing

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:46.690
is exposing that to customers.

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:51.000
So you can bring along with
you any pre-built Docker image.

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:52.750
You can use any
base image you like.

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:55.020
I mentioned before
we default to Ubuntu.

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:58.190
If you want alpine Linux,
bring your alpine Linux.

00:34:58.190 --> 00:35:00.630
You can arbitrary system
libraries, arbitrary language

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:01.440
runtime.

00:35:01.440 --> 00:35:03.030
But everything else is the same.

00:35:03.030 --> 00:35:05.490
You get the same serverless
execution environment.

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:06.810
You don't manage any servers.

00:35:06.810 --> 00:35:08.610
You pay only when
code is executed.

00:35:08.610 --> 00:35:10.320
All the other bells
and whistles that you

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:14.416
saw before-- the scheduling, the
environment variables-- well,

00:35:14.416 --> 00:35:16.540
environment variable, you
can probably do yourself.

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:19.140
But all the surrounding
things are the same.

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:22.380
And you just bring
your container image.

00:35:22.380 --> 00:35:24.690
And to prove that
this is true, I

00:35:24.690 --> 00:35:27.570
want to invite Steren
onstage to give you a demo.

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:30.040
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:33.031 --> 00:35:34.030
STEREN GIANNINI: Thanks.

00:35:34.030 --> 00:35:34.696
Can you hear me?

00:35:34.696 --> 00:35:36.140
Yes.

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:38.240
So we were
brainstorming this demo.

00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:41.220
And why would you use
containers when this here

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:43.060
provides you Node and Python?

00:35:43.060 --> 00:35:47.280
Well, maybe you have your own
favorite programming language,

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:48.460
like GO or REST.

00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:49.600
We tried them.

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:50.470
It works.

00:35:50.470 --> 00:35:51.520
But is that [INAUDIBLE]?

00:35:51.520 --> 00:35:52.420
No.

00:35:52.420 --> 00:35:55.000
Well, what if we
could actually write

00:35:55.000 --> 00:35:58.000
a Cloud Function
that renders a 3D

00:35:58.000 --> 00:36:00.710
image based on a URL parameter?

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:01.930
How cool was that?

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:05.980
And to do that-- if you can
switch to the demo screen--

00:36:05.980 --> 00:36:11.389
to do that, actually, you wrote
a very small HTTP function.

00:36:11.389 --> 00:36:12.430
Let me show you the code.

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:19.010
It's a Python function,
but simply, it

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:22.920
will execute a 3D software
to render an image out

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:25.880
of a predefined 3D model.

00:36:25.880 --> 00:36:31.609
And so this software is not in
the Cloud Function base image,

00:36:31.609 --> 00:36:34.150
the one that you get when you
use the Python version of Cloud

00:36:34.150 --> 00:36:35.440
Function.

00:36:35.440 --> 00:36:39.490
To use this software,
I had to put it

00:36:39.490 --> 00:36:41.440
into the image of my function.

00:36:41.440 --> 00:36:47.860
And to do that, I simply wrote
these 10 lines of code that

00:36:47.860 --> 00:36:52.360
is literally a Docker file,
so that we describe how

00:36:52.360 --> 00:36:54.130
to build that container image.

00:36:54.130 --> 00:36:55.630
So first thing to
note is that it

00:36:55.630 --> 00:36:59.680
starts from the official
Python base image.

00:36:59.680 --> 00:37:01.090
Nothing fancy here.

00:37:01.090 --> 00:37:04.310
Then what I'm doing is I'm
installing a 3D software,

00:37:04.310 --> 00:37:07.840
which is a C++ module
available on Ubuntu.

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:09.820
And then I'm starting my app.

00:37:09.820 --> 00:37:11.500
I'm exposing the right port.

00:37:11.500 --> 00:37:16.000
And I'm starting the
app as it should be.

00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:18.380
OK, let's deploy that.

00:37:18.380 --> 00:37:21.640
So the first step would
be to build this--

00:37:21.640 --> 00:37:25.150
the Docker local file and the
code-- into a container image.

00:37:25.150 --> 00:37:27.430
For the sake of time,
I already did that.

00:37:27.430 --> 00:37:30.670
If I wanted to do it, I
would have use Docker Build.

00:37:33.910 --> 00:37:37.900
Or I could have used Cloud
Build that builds that for you

00:37:37.900 --> 00:37:38.860
in the cloud.

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:41.230
But this image has
already been built.

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:45.475
Now, I'm just going to
deploy it to Cloud Functions.

00:37:45.475 --> 00:37:49.120
Click Cloud, Functions, Deploy.

00:37:49.120 --> 00:37:50.380
You know that part.

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:51.570
The new thing is --image.

00:37:54.370 --> 00:37:58.280
And then I point to
my container image

00:37:58.280 --> 00:38:00.205
that I stored in
Google Cloud registry.

00:38:07.825 --> 00:38:08.803
Looks good.

00:38:11.737 --> 00:38:12.730
Here we are.

00:38:12.730 --> 00:38:16.070
We are deploying the
image to Cloud Function,

00:38:16.070 --> 00:38:21.080
so it should take
around 30 minutes.

00:38:21.080 --> 00:38:22.680
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:30.370
As you see, I could have
used any programming language

00:38:30.370 --> 00:38:33.490
I want, as long as I can build
it into a container image.

00:38:33.490 --> 00:38:36.310
I could have used any
OS package I want,

00:38:36.310 --> 00:38:41.950
as long as it is
available in my image.

00:38:41.950 --> 00:38:43.570
Or even I could
bring my own binary.

00:38:43.570 --> 00:38:46.120
We have customers who
have special binaries

00:38:46.120 --> 00:38:48.557
that they need to import
into their Cloud Function.

00:38:48.557 --> 00:38:49.390
That's what they do.

00:38:49.390 --> 00:38:51.610
They use containers to do that.

00:38:51.610 --> 00:38:52.880
Here we have it.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:55.420
So let's click on it.

00:38:55.420 --> 00:38:58.850
So as I told you, this
function takes a URL parameter,

00:38:58.850 --> 00:39:01.450
which is the location.

00:39:01.450 --> 00:39:04.270
Because as you know, we
have many Cloud Next coming.

00:39:04.270 --> 00:39:07.620
